🔑 Expanded Standard Flow (Linux 8 Boot Process)

Firmware (BIOS/UEFI)
=====================

* Runs POST (Power‑On Self Test): checks CPU, RAM, keyboard, storage controllers, and the RTC (Real‑Time Clock).
* If any device fails, firmware throws an error immediately (beep codes or error message) and halts boot.
* If everything is fine, firmware looks for the boot partition / EFI System Partition on the selected boot device.
* Once found, it passes control to GRUB2.


Boot Loader (GRUB2)
========================

GRUB2 presents a menu of available kernels and boot options.
You can see multiple kernel versions here and select the one you want (latest, older fallback, rescue mode).
Loads the chosen kernel (vmlinuz) and initramfs into memory.
Passes kernel parameters (e.g., root=UUID=..., systemd.unit=rescue.target).


Kernel Initialization
==========================

Decompression: The kernel image is compressed; it unpacks itself into RAM and begins execution.
Core setup: Initializes CPU scheduling, memory management, and interrupt handling.
Driver & module loading: Loads built‑in drivers and kernel modules from /lib/modules/<version>/ to enable hardware (disk, network, USB, graphics).
Temporary root: Uses initramfs to mount a temporary root filesystem until the real root is found.
If drivers are missing or root UUID is wrong, you’ll see a kernel panic.


Initramfs (Dracut in RHEL 8)
================================
Provides essential drivers for storage, RAID, LVM, encryption.
Runs scripts to locate and mount the actual root filesystem.
Once successful, the kernel switches from initramfs to the real root.


systemd Initialization (PID 1)
=================================
systemd replaces the old SysV init system.
Reads the default target (multi-user.target or graphical.target).
Starts services in parallel using unit files (.service, .target, .mount).
Handles dependencies, logging, and recovery (rescue/emergency mode).


Login Stage
================
Text mode: getty starts on virtual terminals and provides a username/password prompt on the console.
Graphical mode: A Display Manager (like GDM, LightDM, or SDDM) provides a GUI login screen.
Once you log in, the shell or desktop environment starts, and the system is fully ready for use
